# The reconciler of a repository's charters, for the repository `verse` (git-issue dzxn44gw;
# the design is j5vbwvja and its parts, and the decision on j5vbwvja: the gate is the install,
# not the merge). RENDERED by system/repocharters from charter.tony.tmpl: edit the template.
#
# MAIN, in what follows and in the records' names, is the branch the repository's charters
# follow, which for this rendering is `main`.
#
# WHAT IT KEEPS TRUE: what runs in verse is the last state of the repository's charters directory
# on main that an ask answered yes to, however it reached main. These records keep the states
# apart, and every rule's subject is one of them or an effect of one:
#
#   repo-charters.verse.state.main           what is on main: {sha, tree}
#   repo-charters.verse.state.live           what refs/verse/live holds: {sha, tree, from}
#   repo-charters.verse.candidate.<tree>     one per charters tree ever on main or proposed by
#                                                a pull request to main: {tree, ask, title, seen},
#                                                `sha` where main carries it, `checked` once
#                                                checked, then `answer` and `askedAt`
#   repo-charters.verse.verdict.<tree>       what verse says about the tree on a pull request
#                                                that proposes it: {decision, check, text}
#   repo-charters.verse.pull.<number>        what an open pull request to main proposes now:
#                                                {pr, head, tree}, `tree` empty for nothing
#   repo-charters.verse.livecheck.live       the minute's check of refs/verse/live against what
#                                                is installed, and the git io's answer beside it
#   repo-charters.verse.running              verse's note of its last install: {sha, tree, from}
#
# A tree is the sha of the charters directory's tree object, as the git io reflects it with
# `-tree <path>` (`tree.charters` on every ref): the identity of the directory's contents, the
# same across commits that differ elsewhere and after a squash. The answer stands for the tree, so
# a change answered yes once installs on every commit that carries it, a rollback included, with
# no second ask.
#
# THE EMPTY TREE (4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904) is the directory with nothing in it, or gone: a state like any
# other. Its question asks to remove every charter at `repos.verse`, and installing it does.
#
# THE ASK is a task's: the propose rule spawns a script whose one call is `question-ask`, so the question
# is on the docket as any ask is, answerable from the CLI, Slack, or the pull request below, and
# its answer is the task's result, written onto the candidate. Its id is the task's with the
# number of the call that asks, its second action after the script itself:
# `repo-charters.verse:propose@repo-charters.verse.candidate.<tree>~2`, which is how the pull
# request names it.
#
# THE ANSWER is read as text, `<principal> chose <option>` with `: <words>` after it: the option is
# the word after the principal, so the words never read as a choice (system/repocharters,
# answeredProceed).
#
# ON THE PULL REQUEST TO MAIN, first: a pull request to main that changes the charters (the github
# io's `-tree`, against the head's merge base) proposes the tree at its head, and the question is
# asked there, where the change is reviewed, beside its `verse` status (the answer) and its
# `verse/check` status (the check) on the head. Verse holds nothing: the merge is the repository's,
# under whatever review it asks for, and the gate is the install. Neither status can be a required
# check: verse says them only on a pull request that changes the charters, and a required check
# would hold every other pull request to main. What it says follows the tree the pull request
# proposes NOW: pushed on to another tree, it is asked about that one in the same comment; once it
# leaves the charters alone, the comment says so. The answer stands for the tree, so a yes given
# on the pull request installs it once it merges, with no second question, and a tree merged
# unanswered is asked on the candidate's issue.
#
# THE CANDIDATE'S ISSUE is for a change that reached main without that answer (an ordinary push, a
# pull request merged around verse): the question is asked on an issue, where the repository's
# people read, saying what main carries against what runs and how to see the difference, with the
# answer heard there and verse's commit status on main's commit saying what verse waits for and
# then what it decided. It is opened only once verse/live stands, while the candidate is main's
# and not what verse/live holds, closed as superseded when main moves on, and closed once its tree
# is installed; its question stands either way. A no closes it with the commit status failed.
#
# THE CHECK is apart from the decision and from the install: each candidate tree is checked against
# what runs by the verse's charter check, which installs nothing, and its verdict is a status of
# its own on either pull request (`verse/check`); while main's tree would be refused an issue says
# so, and it closes when main's tree passes.
#
# verse/live IS A RECORD, `refs/verse/live` (iod reference, a record): a chain of commits the git
# io writes, each holding a charters tree as its whole tree, its parent the entry before, and the
# commit of main it came from as its `From:` trailer. Not a branch: nothing checks it out or
# builds it, and `git log refs/verse/live` is what ran, when, and from where. The tree is its
# identity, as it is everywhere here: an entry is appended only for a tree the last does not hold.
#
# THE INSTALL FOLLOWS verse/live, which is what runs: the git io reflects the record, and a tree
# it holds whose candidate was answered yes is installed, by the git io, from its own copy of the
# repository, at `repos.verse`, as itself, pruning what the tree leaves out (iod reference, the
# out half of git). So a commit to main that leaves the charters alone changes nothing the install
# reads, and installs nothing. A yes on main's tree appends it to verse/live (the git io, as the
# App): the record made at the first install, appended to on a later yes or a rollback. The io's
# status says what it installed, for which entry and from which commit, and the rules read that:
# what runs is recorded, the candidate's issue is closed, and the commit status on main's commit
# passes. An install the io refuses is a GitHub issue, closed by the next install that succeeds,
# and the commit status fails. A tree on verse/live nobody answered yes to is not installed.
#
# A REFUSED INSTALL IS ASKED AGAIN when the verse moves, not only when the repository does: what
# refused it may have been the verse's (no runner offering a spawn yet, a build behind the
# charters, an io not registered), and the minute's check of verse/live finds when the tree would
# install. Each time that check's verdict begins to be `installs` for an entry of verse/live, the
# install of what the entry holds is asked again, once; the io does nothing for an install that
# stands.
#
# NOTHING STANDS UNSAID. Each way main, verse/live and the installed charters can disagree has an
# outcome somebody sees:
#
#   main's tree unanswered          its question, on the candidate's issue
#   main's tree answered no         an issue, open while main holds it
#   main's check refused            an issue, open until main's tree passes
#   an install refused              an issue, open until an install succeeds, and the install
#                                   asked again when the minute's check says it would
#   verse/live not what runs        an issue, open until verse/live is back in step: the installed
#                                   charters are checked against verse/live's tree every minute
#                                   (the reconciler's own schedule), and two rounds differing at
#                                   one commit open it, so an install under way is not taken for
#                                   it. Not restored: the ask comes with a pull request to fix it.
#                                   A verse/live that is gone is the same issue.
#   a question nothing proposes     settled `obsolete` by the git io's sweep, every minute, and
#                                   asked again if its tree is proposed again
#
# THIS INSTANCE: the git io reflects the repository as `git.verse` with `-tree` naming the
# charters directory and `-out`, the github io reflects signadot/verse with the same `-tree` and reads
# its out place, and the git io owns `repos.verse` or holds `child-owner` above it, since it
# installs as itself.

# ONE PACKAGE PER REPOSITORY, at `repo-charters.<repo>`, so a deployment reconciling two
# repositories installs two packages, and every outbox below is a plain name inside the package:
# a `from` is relative, and one spelled with the repository's id in front (`verse.install`) reads
# as verse's own half when the repository is called `verse`.
at: repo-charters.verse
reads: [git.verse, github.signadot.verse, verse.schedule]
# Every minute: verse/live is checked against what is installed, and the questions about trees
# nothing proposes any more are settled `obsolete`.
schedules:
- name: minute
  every: 1m
# The outboxes are places inside the package, which is where the rules below derive the inputs.
sends:
- from: install
  to: git.verse.out
- from: check
  to: git.verse.out
- from: livecheck
  to: git.verse.out
- from: out.git
  to: git.verse.out
- from: out.github
  to: github.signadot.verse.out
# The levels, lowest first: what the git io reflects, the pull requests the github io reflects,
# what is on main and on verse/live, the checks and their verdicts, the candidates, what verse
# says about each tree on a pull request, what each pull request proposes now, the minute's ticks,
# the check of verse/live against what is installed, the install the git io is asked for and its
# answer, what runs, and the effects. A derive lands strictly
# above its rule, and a read inside a derive at or below it.
strata:
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match: {path: git}
- name: pulls
  level: 1
  match: {path: "github.signadot.verse.pr"}
- name: state
  level: 2
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.state"}
- name: checks
  level: 3
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.check"}
- name: candidates
  level: 5
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate"}
- name: verdicts
  level: 6
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"}
- name: proposals
  level: 7
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.pull"}
- name: ticks
  level: 8
  match: {path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.verse:minute".tick'}
- name: livecheck
  level: 9
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck"}
- name: install
  level: 10
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.install"}
- name: running
  level: 20
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.running"}
- name: out
  level: 30
  match: {path: "repo-charters.verse.out"}
rules:
# What is on main: its sha and the charters tree, a candidate per tree ever seen there, and the
# check of that tree.
- name: main
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "git.verse.ref.main"
      value: {tree: {charters: !glob "*"}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.state"
      id: main
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree.charters}
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree.charters
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree.charters}
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.check"
      id: $subject.value.tree.charters
      value: {to: "check.repos.verse", tree: $subject.value.tree.charters, sha: $subject.value.sha}
# What verse/live holds, which is what runs once installed: its last entry, the charters tree it
# holds, and the commit of main the tree came from. An entry the git io wrote names that commit;
# anybody who can push can write one that does not, and its `from` is empty: what it holds is
# what verse/live holds all the same, with no commit of main to say statuses on.
- name: live
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "git.verse.record.live"
      value: {tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.state"
      id: live
      value: {sha: $subject.value.sha, tree: $subject.value.tree, from: $subject.value.from}
# An open pull request to main that changes the charters proposes the tree at its head: a
# candidate and its check. The github io's `-tree` reports `changes` against the head's merge
# base with main, so a pull request that leaves the charters alone proposes nothing, and neither
# does one branched before main's charters last changed; one that removes the directory proposes
# the empty tree. What each open pull request to main proposes NOW is a record of its own,
# `pull.<number>`, with an empty `tree` where it proposes nothing: a pull request moves between
# trees, and what verse says on it follows the tree it proposes, not one it proposed before.
- name: pull
  stratum: pulls
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "github.signadot.verse.pr.*"
      value: {state: open, base: "main", headSha: !glob "*", changes: {charters: !glob "*"}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.changes.charters
      value: {tree: $subject.value.changes.charters, seen: $subject.value.updatedAt}
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.check"
      id: $subject.value.changes.charters
      value: {to: "check.repos.verse", tree: $subject.value.changes.charters, sha: $subject.value.headSha}
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.pull"
      id: $subject.value.number
      value: {pr: $subject.value.number, head: $subject.value.headSha, tree: $subject.value.changes.charters}
- name: pull-quiet
  stratum: pulls
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "github.signadot.verse.pr.*"
      value: !and [{state: open, base: "main", headSha: !glob "*"}, !not.has-path changes.charters]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.pull"
      id: $subject.value.number
      value: {pr: $subject.value.number, head: $subject.value.headSha, tree: ""}
# THE CHECK, apart from the decision: the git io asks the verse's charter check what an install
# of a tree would do (iod reference, the out half of git), and its verdict is written onto the
# candidate, where the pull requests' `verse/check` statuses are drawn from. The check input
# carries the commit the tree was seen at (`sha`), so a new commit asks again: a pull request's
# tree can reach the git io before its copy has fetched the branch.
- name: checked
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.check.*"
      value: {verdict: installs, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {checked: installs}
- name: check-refused
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.check.*"
      value: {verdict: refused, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {checked: refused, refusal: $subject.value.refusal}
# ONE STANDING ISSUE says main's charters would be refused, and it is closed when main passes:
# opened (or reopened) when the check of main's tree is refused, saying which tree and why, and
# closed when the check of main's tree finds it installs. One issue rather than one per tree,
# because closing is a write onto the issue's input, and a rule can name one input but not every
# tree that ever failed.
- name: main-refused
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.check.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {verdict: refused, tree: .[mainTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: check-refused
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: main's charters would be refused"
          body: "verse's charter check says an install of the charters on main (tree $subject.value.tree) into repos.verse would be refused:\n\n$subject.value.refusal\n\nThis issue closes when the charters on main pass the check."
        closed: false
- name: main-passed
  stratum: checks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.check.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {verdict: installs, tree: .[mainTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: check-refused
      value: {closed: true}
# EACH CANDIDATE IS NAMED for its question and its pull request's title: a tree is installed,
# and the empty tree, the charters directory with nothing in it or gone, is every charter at
# repos.verse removed. Named once, latched on the name.
- name: name-tree
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path ask, {tree: !not "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}]
  action:
    patch:
      ask: "Install the charters tree $subject.value.tree into repos.verse?"
      title: "install the charters tree $subject.value.tree"
      seen: "1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"
- name: name-removal
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path ask, {tree: "4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904"}]
  action:
    patch:
      ask: "Remove every charter at repos.verse? The repository's charters directory holds none."
      title: "remove every charter at repos.verse"
      seen: "1970-01-01T00:00:00Z"
# A candidate nobody has answered is asked about: a task whose one call is the ask, so the
# question is the task's and its answer is the task's result, written onto the candidate. The
# answer stands for the TREE, whichever commit carries it from then on. The question's id is the
# task's with its first call's number, which is how the pull requests below name it.
#
# OBSOLETE IS THE THIRD ANSWER: the minute's sweep has the git io settle a question whose tree
# nothing proposes any more (`obsolete-sweep`), so no question stands for nothing. Such a tree is
# asked about again once something proposes it again: once main holds it, or once a pull request
# proposing it moves on from when it was asked. A pull request writes `seen`, its `updatedAt`, and
# the answer records `askedAt`, the `seen` it was asked at (a candidate is named with a floor).
- name: propose
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - {ask: !glob "*", seen: !glob "*"}
        - !or
          - !not.has-path answer
          - {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose obsolete(: |\\z)", tree: .[mainTree]}
          - {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose obsolete(: |\\z)", seen: !gt.get-path(root) askedAt}
  action:
    spawn:
      autonomy:
      - match: {system: question}
        then: allow
      - then: deny
      environment:
        # The question as the script's environment: its text is the candidate's, and spliced
        # into the script it would be a program.
        vars: {ASK: $[ask]}
        systems:
          network: {mode: open}
          question: {}
      script: |
        verse-call-tool question-ask -question "$ASK" -options proceed -options deny -options obsolete
    onDone:
      patch: {answer: $result, askedAt: $subject.value.seen}
# WHAT VERSE SAYS ABOUT A TREE on any pull request that proposes it, from its answer and its
# check: the decision (the `verse` status) and the check (the `verse/check` status, and the
# comment's text, which says why a refused tree would be refused). Neither holds the merge.
# A NEW TREE'S VERDICT is written whole, pending on both counts, in one derive: the rule drawing
# a pull request from it reads every field, and one that found half a verdict would fail and
# wait to be tried again. After that each count is its own: the answer rewrites the decision,
# the check the check and the text.
- name: verdict-new
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      # Only as the candidate appears, before it is named: a firing writes after the commit that
      # caused it, so one that re-fired on every later change could land after the answer or the
      # check and put its pending fields back.
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, !not.has-path checked, !not.has-path ask]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: pending
          description: "not answered yet; merging does not wait for it"
        check: {state: pending, description: "checking against what runs"}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. A yes to the question below installs them once this merges; merging does not wait for it, and unanswered, the question is asked again on an issue after the merge."
- name: verdict-yes
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose proceed(: |\\z)"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: success
          description: "answered yes; installs once merged"
- name: verdict-no
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose deny(: |\\z)"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        decision:
          state: failure
          description: "answered no; not installed, merged or not"
- name: verdict-installs
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: {checked: installs}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        check: {state: success, description: "installs cleanly over what runs"}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. A yes to the question below installs them once this merges; merging does not wait for it, and unanswered, the question is asked again on an issue after the merge."
- name: verdict-refused
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: {checked: refused}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        check: {state: failure, description: "would be refused; see the comment"}
        text: "This pull request changes the charters verse runs for this repository. A yes to the question below installs them once this merges; merging does not wait for it, and unanswered, the question is asked again on an issue after the merge.\n\nverse's charter check says these charters would be refused if installed into repos.verse:\n\n$subject.value.refusal"
# ON THE PULL REQUEST TO MAIN, what verse says about the tree it proposes now: the comment with
# the question, and both statuses on its head, drawn from that tree's verdict. Re drawn when any
# verdict changes (`when`), since what is read in an action is copied, not followed. One input per
# pull request, so a pull request that moves to another tree is asked about that tree in the same
# comment.
- name: pr-asked
  stratum: proposals
  condition:
    when:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.verdict.*"
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.pull.*"
      value: {tree: !not ""}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github.pr"
      id: $subject.value.pr
      value:
        to: "pr.$subject.value.pr"
        tree: $subject.value.tree
        text: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: text}
        asks: "repo-charters.verse:propose@repo-charters.verse.candidate.$subject.value.tree~2"
        commitStatus:
          verse:
            state: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: decision.state}
            description: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: decision.description}
            sha: $subject.value.head
          "verse/check":
            state: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: check.state}
            description: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, verdict, $subject.value.tree], path: check.description}
            sha: $subject.value.head
# A pull request that no longer changes the charters is asked nothing: the comment says so, and
# both statuses on its head say there is nothing to wait for. One that never did writes an input
# with no `to`, which no io reads.
- name: pr-quiet
  stratum: proposals
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.pull.*"
      value: {tree: ""}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github.pr"
      id: $subject.value.pr
      value:
        tree: ""
        text: "This pull request no longer changes the charters verse runs, so verse asks nothing here."
        asks: []
        commitStatus:
          verse: {state: success, description: "changes no charters", sha: $subject.value.head}
          "verse/check": {state: success, description: "changes no charters", sha: $subject.value.head}
# ON THE CANDIDATE'S ISSUE, for a change that reached main without an answer: while a candidate is
# asked about, its question rides an issue saying what main carries against what runs, with the
# answer heard there, and verse's commit status pending on main's commit. Only once verse/live
# stands, and only while the candidate is main's and not what verse/live holds; one main has moved
# past is closed as superseded, and one installed is closed by the install.
- name: candidate-issue
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: sha}
        # A read of what does not stand matches nothing, so this rule waits for verse/live.
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - !not.has-path answer
        # The commit is read so that main moving to a new commit of this tree redraws the commit status.
        - {tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha], title: !glob "*"}
        - {tree: !not .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: $subject.value.title"
          body: "main at $subject.value.sha carries the charters tree $subject.value.tree, which is not what runs at repos.verse, and nobody has answered for it yet. An answer here installs it.\n\nWhat it changes, against what runs:\n\n    git fetch origin refs/verse/live\n    git diff FETCH_HEAD $subject.value.tree\n\nThis issue closes once the tree is installed, or once main carries another."
        text: "What is on main against what runs. An answer here installs it."
        asks: "repo-charters.verse:propose@repo-charters.verse.candidate.$subject.value.tree~2"
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: pending, description: "waiting for an answer to the question on the candidate's issue", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
        closed: false
# The check's verdict on main's commit of a tree main carries, while it is asked about on the
# candidate's issue.
- name: live-checked
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, {checked: installs, sha: !glob "*"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {"verse/check": {state: success, description: "installs cleanly over what runs", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
- name: live-check-refused
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !and [!not.has-path answer, {checked: refused, sha: !glob "*"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {"verse/check": {state: failure, description: "would be refused; see the issue", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
- name: candidate-superseded
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - !not.has-path answer
        - {tree: !not .[mainTree], sha: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {closed: true}
# A no closes the candidate's issue, with verse's commit status failed on main's commit: the record
# of the decision, where the question was asked.
- name: candidate-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose deny(: |\\z)", sha: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        closed: true
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: failure, description: "denied: $subject.value.answer", sha: $subject.value.sha}}
# A yes on main's tree makes verse/live hold it: the record made holding it where verse/live does
# not stand yet (the first install), and the tree appended where it holds another (a later yes,
# or a rollback to a tree answered before), each entry naming the candidate's commit. Two inputs
# for the one record, because a read of verse/live is nothing before it stands, and a rule reading
# nothing never fires.
- name: live-made
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: sha}
        in: {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose proceed(: |\\z)", tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.git"
      id: live-made
      value: {to: "record.live", tree: $subject.value.tree, from: $subject.value.sha, create: true}
- name: live-moved
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        - mainSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: sha}
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: tree}
        in: !and
        - {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose proceed(: |\\z)", tree: .[mainTree], sha: .[mainSha]}
        - {tree: !not .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.git"
      id: live
      value: {to: "record.live", tree: $subject.value.tree, from: $subject.value.sha}
# The tree verse/live holds is installed, where its candidate was answered yes: the git io is
# asked to install it, for verse/live's entry. The input is named for the tree and carries the
# entry and the commit of main it came from, so the io does nothing for a tree it already
# installed for that entry, and a commit to main moves nothing here: only verse/live moving does.
- name: install
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: tree}
        in: {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose proceed(: |\\z)", tree: .[liveTree]}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.install"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        to: "install.repos.verse"
        tree: $subject.value.tree
        sha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: sha}
        from: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: from}
# The io's answer: the tree it installed, for which entry and from which commit. What runs is
# recorded, the candidate's issue is closed, and the commit status on main's commit passes. ONLY
# THE ANSWER FOR verse/live's ENTRY: a rule fires for every status it selects at every commit
# under its place, and what runs is one record, so the statuses of earlier installs must not be
# selected, or whichever fired last would be what runs.
- name: installed
  stratum: install
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.install.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: sha}
        in: !and
        - {address: !glob "*", tree: !glob "*", sha: .[liveSha]}
        - !not.has-path error
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse"
      id: running
      value: {tree: $subject.value.tree, sha: $subject.value.sha, from: $subject.value.from}
    # Merged at depth onto the candidate's issue input, where one was opened, closing it; where
    # none was (the first install, a rollback), this stands alone with no to, and no io reads it.
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: success, description: "installed into repos.verse", sha: $subject.value.from}}
        closed: true
    # A refused install's issue is closed by an install that succeeds.
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: install-failed
      value: {closed: true}
# An install the io refused for good is an issue in the repository, saying what the verse said,
# and the commit status on main's commit fails. The issue and the status stand until an install
# succeeds: a fix on main is a new tree, a new candidate and a new question, and a verse that has
# moved under the tree is the install asked again (install-again).
- name: install-failed
  stratum: install
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.install.*"
      value: {failed: permanent, tree: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    # ONE STANDING ISSUE for a refused install, as for a refused check: opened (or reopened)
    # here, saying which tree and commit and why, and closed by the next install that succeeds.
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: install-failed
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: the install of what verse/live holds failed"
          body: "verse refused to install the charters tree $subject.value.tree, refs/verse/live's entry $subject.value.sha, into repos.verse:\n\n$subject.value.error\n\nverse asks again when its minute check finds the tree would install. This issue closes when an install succeeds."
        closed: false
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value:
        commitStatus: {verse: {state: failure, description: "the install failed; see the issue", sha: $subject.value.from}}
# MAIN'S CHARTERS ANSWERED NO: what runs stays as it was, and an issue says so while main holds the
# denied tree. It closes when main's charters are anything else.
- name: main-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: {tree: .[mainTree], answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose deny(: |\\z)"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: main-denied
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: main's charters were answered no"
          body: "The charters on main (tree $subject.value.tree) were answered no: $subject.value.answer.\n\nWhat runs at repos.verse stays as it was. This issue closes when main's charters change."
        closed: false
- name: main-not-denied
  stratum: candidates
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.candidate.*"
      value: !let
        let:
        - mainTree: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, main], path: tree}
        in: !and [{tree: .[mainTree]}, !not {answer: !regexp "^\\S+ chose deny(: |\\z)"}]
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: main-denied
      value: {closed: true}
# EVERY MINUTE, verse/live against what is installed: the git io reads the record in its own copy
# and asks the verse's charter check what installing its tree would change (iod reference, the
# check of a record). Only once verse/live has stood: before the first install there is nothing
# to compare.
- name: live-check
  stratum: ticks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.verse:minute".tick.*'
      value: !let
        let:
        - liveSha: !get-path {at: [repo-charters, verse, state, live], path: sha}
        in: {due: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck"
      id: live
      value: {to: "check.repos.verse", record: live, round: $subject.value.due}
# A REFUSED INSTALL ASKED AGAIN: each time the minute's check finds that what an entry of
# verse/live holds would install, where it did not find so before, the install of that tree is
# asked again, once. What refused it may have been the verse's and not the tree's: no runner
# offering a spawn yet, a build behind the charters, an io not registered. The io says since when
# the verdict has held at the entry (`verdictSince`), which is the same round after round while it
# holds and begins again at a new entry, so the install's input changes once each time the verdict
# begins and not once a minute; and the io does nothing for an install that stands, so a verdict
# over a tree that runs costs nothing. Not an edge: an edge rises only where the verdict was not
# `installs` before, so a new entry found to install, as the one before it was, would never be
# asked again.
- name: install-again
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck.*"
      value: {record: live, verdict: installs, tree: !glob "*", verdictSince: !glob "*"}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.install"
      id: $subject.value.tree
      value: {again: $subject.value.verdictSince}
# verse/live HOLDING SOMETHING OTHER THAN WHAT RUNS is an open issue: not restored. The ask comes
# when somebody opens a pull request to fix it; what runs back in step closes it. Two rounds in a
# row at one entry, so an install verse is making is not taken for it.
- name: live-in-step
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck.*"
      value: {record: live, verdict: installs, changes: 0}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value: {closed: true}
- name: live-drifted
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck.*"
      value: {record: live, verdict: installs, changes: !gt 0, differingFor: !ge 2}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "refs/verse/live (entry $subject.value.sha, tree $subject.value.tree) differs from the charters installed at repos.verse by $subject.value.changes charter(s).\n\nOpen a pull request to main to change the charters, or put what runs back in step with it; this issue closes when they agree."
        closed: false
- name: live-refused
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck.*"
      value: {record: live, verdict: refused, differingFor: !ge 2}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "refs/verse/live (entry $subject.value.sha, tree $subject.value.tree) holds charters an install at repos.verse would refuse:\n\n$subject.value.refusal\n\nOpen a pull request to main to change the charters; this issue closes when what it holds would install and runs."
        closed: false
- name: live-missing
  stratum: livecheck
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repo-charters.verse.livecheck.*"
      value: {record: live, verdict: missing}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.github"
      id: live-drift
      value:
        to: issue
        open:
          title: "charters: verse/live is not what runs"
          body: "refs/verse/live is gone from the repository, so nothing records what runs at repos.verse there.\n\nPut it back at the entry verse wrote last; this issue closes when it is."
        closed: false
# EVERY MINUTE, the questions about trees nothing proposes now are settled `obsolete` by the git io:
# what is proposed is main's tree, verse/live's, and each open pull request to main's change, read
# at the tick, and only questions posed by the tick are settled.
- name: obsolete-sweep
  stratum: ticks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: 'verse.schedule."repo-charters.verse:minute".tick.*'
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repo-charters.verse.out.git"
      id: obsolete
      value:
        to: obsolete
        prefix: "repo-charters.verse:propose@repo-charters.verse.candidate."
        before: $subject.value.due
        keep:
          state: !list-paths {at: "repo-charters.verse.state.*", path: tree}
          pulls: !list-paths {at: "github.signadot.verse.pr.*", value: {state: open, base: "main"}, path: changes.charters}
